[38;5;252m[1m## One finding worth your attention[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;10mbad(data)[0m writes [38;5;10mbuffer[data] = 1[0m into a 10-element stack array with only a lower-bound check, so [38;5;10mdata >= 10[0m is undefined behavior in the C. Measured against the compiled C library, those inputs are not merely unspecified — they are unstable: [38;5;10mbad(12)[0m–[38;5;10mbad(15)[0m, [38;5;10mbad(20)[0m–[38;5;10mbad(23)[0m, and [38;5;10mbad(4096)[0m segfault, while [38;5;10mbad(10)[0m, [38;5;10mbad(11)[0m, [38;5;10mbad(16)[0m–[38;5;10mbad(39)[0m, [38;5;10mbad(1000)[0m return normally. That pattern is an artifact of GCC's stack layout at this optimization level and would shift with a different compiler, flag set, or ASLR draw.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The Rust translation handles this by allocating a 1024-element slack region after [38;5;10mbuffer[0m so the stray store lands in unused space. That reproduces the [3mbenign[23m outcome, which matches C for many out-of-range indices but not the crashing ones. No translation can match both, since the C behavior is not a function of the input alone. The equivalence tests therefore assert on the defined domain ([38;5;10mdata <= 9[0m, all values of [38;5;10mgood[0m's argument, all [38;5;10mdriver[0m pairs with [38;5;10mbadData <= 9[0m); [38;5;10mbad_out_of_bounds_is_undefined_in_c[0m pins the [38;5;10mbad(9)[0m boundary and documents the reasoning. If your harness exercises [38;5;10mbad[0m with indices ≥ 10, expect divergence there, and it is inherent rather than fixable.[0m[0m
